 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-012\", \"clauses\": {\"read_and_write\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"reasoning\": \"F11 is the write half of the clause: the learner sets the clock to a time drawn from the time_str domain. The read half is produced by F12 (What time does the clock show?), so the clause is met across the two families. F11 is named because it is the only family in which the learner writes time.\"}, \"time\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"What time does the clock show?\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 asks the learner to read the time the clock shows, so the object of the clause, time, is what the problem asks for.\"}, \"hour\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F08\", \"template\": \"{hour}:00\", \"reasoning\": \"F08 is the keyed answer for an hour-only time. Its hour slot is a number in the range 1 to 12 (bounds['hour'], lo 1, hi 12), which is the hour range the clause names.\"}, \"half_hour\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"reasoning\": \"The time_str domain of F11 is repeats a generated value, and its observed printed values include several times whose minutes are 30. A half-hour time is therefore produced in the question.\"}, \"quarter_hour\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"Set the clock to show {time_str}{trailing}\", \"reasoning\": \"The observed printed values of time_str in F11 include times whose minutes are 15 and 45, so quarter-hour times are produced in the question.\"}, \"analog_clock\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"What time does the clock show?\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 refers to the clock the learner reads, and the hint families F01 to F06 describe its long and short hands. The analog clock is the representation the problem shows.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F05\", \"F09\", \"F13\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"The short (hour) hand points near {hour}.\", \"{hour}:{minute:02d}\", \"{hours}:{minutes:02d}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F09 (correct_answer, format_data.mcq_options, format_data.time_str) fills minute from a number in the range 0 to 59 (basis: a minute of the hour), with no restriction to the hour, half hour and quarter hour precisions. So it can print a minute that is not 0, 15, 30 or 45, which is outside the competency's named scope. F13 (same three fields) fills hours from a number in the range 0 to 23 (basis: an hour of the day). That can print hour 0 or hours 13 to 23, which cannot be shown on a 12-number analog clock and is beyond grade 1. Its minutes slot is the same 0 to 59 range, so F13 also prints minutes outside the scope. F05 (hints) fills hour from the repeats a generated value domain, with printed_runs 1 to 12, and has no template chosen by minute. So it prints the hour number for every minute, including minute 45 (and 30), where the short hand is nearer the next number. The hint then says the short hand points near the earlier number, which is a misleading statement. F01 to F04, F06, F07, F08, F10, F11 and F12 keep to the hour, half-hour and quarter-hour scope, and their observed values stay within that scope. The clauses are all producible; the failures are the scope-breaking minute and hour domains in F09 and F13 and the misleading hour hint in F05.\"}",
